comdlg32: file dialog follow-ups -- clickable breadcrumb segments; nav arrows drawn at the dialog's dpi

Two follow-ups to the 11.10-85 restyle of the Common Item Dialog (ROADMAP.md item 1a), in
the operator's words while daily-driving 11.10-89-r2:

  1. "we should add the ability to click on the specific part of the tree as a secondary
     method of navigation aside from the arrow buttons next to it."
  2. "the Arrow icons themselves are currently too small and positioned too high of center."

CLICKABLE BREADCRUMB. neutron_update_crumbs() now keeps the IShellItem for each segment
next to its name; the painter lays each ancestor segment out as a hit rect (the text plus
5px of air, as tall as a nav button, centred in the band) and paints the hot one with the
same round NEUTRON_NAV_HOT / NEUTRON_NAV_PRESSED fill the nav buttons use. The dialog
handles WM_MOUSEMOVE / WM_MOUSELEAVE / WM_LBUTTONDOWN / WM_LBUTTONUP / WM_CAPTURECHANGED /
WM_SETCURSOR (hand cursor) for the band, and a release on the pressed segment calls
IExplorerBrowser_BrowseToObject() on that segment's item -- the same call the Up button
and SetFolder go through, so nothing about what the dialog returns changes. The nav arrows
stay; this is the secondary method the operator asked for. The current folder (last
segment) is not a target; the ellipsis is not a target.

NAV ARROWS. MEASURED: the mockup (itemdlg.html, .tbtn) is a 2.6em x 2em button (34x26 at
its 13px em) with the glyph flex-centred in it; in the 1134px render the arrow glyph
centres sit on the band centre to within 0.5px (glyph cy 120.0-120.5, band 84..155). The
11.10-85 captures at 96 dpi are also centred (glyph cy 81-82 in a 50..114 band) and the
glyph is 12x12 px. So at 96 dpi there is no fault -- and both of the operator's prefixes
run at LogPixels = 0xa3 = 163 (`neutron prefix provision` writes round(96 * 1.7)).

At 163 dpi the code path is (comctl32/toolbar.c, read, not run): TB_ADDBITMAP of a
standard strip fixes the bitmap at 24x24 and the image is drawn at that native size;
TB_SETBUTTONSIZE is clamped to padding + bitmap, i.e. max(51,31) x max(44,30) = 51x44;
TOOLBAR_DrawButton centres the image horizontally but top-anchors it at padding.cy/2 =
3px. Net: a 12px glyph in a 51x44 button (0.27 of the height where the mockup and the
96-dpi build have ~0.4) whose centre sits at y=15 of 44 -- 7px above the button centre.
Too small and too high, at every dpi above 96.

Fix, LOCAL to this dialog (the strip is shared by every toolbar in every app and is left
alone): on_wm_notify() draws the three glyphs itself from the strip's own path data
(build/art/gen_toolbar_icons.py: HIST_BACK, HIST_FORWARD, VIEW_PARENTFOLDER in a 24-box,
stroke 2, round caps/joins), scaled to the dialog's dpi, 6x supersampled with a geometric
pen, box-filtered to coverage and GdiAlphaBlend'ed centred in the button, and returns
CDRF_SKIPDEFAULT. init_toolbar() declares a 1px bitmap so the clamp no longer forces
31x30, and asks for the mockup's 34x26 (dpi-scaled). Colour is the mockup's .tbtn
#b9bec4 (NEUTRON_NAV_GLYPH). At 96 dpi the glyph is the same 12px design in a 34x26
button instead of 31x30; at 163 dpi it is 20px in a 58x44 button, centred.

Kill switches (no rebuild): NEUTRON_CRUMB_CLICK=0 makes the breadcrumb static text again;
NEUTRON_NAV_GLYPH=0 restores comctl32's native-size bitmaps and the 30x26 request.
NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 prints, at the outcome, `neutron-crumb-nav: segment N <name> -> hr X`
after each breadcrumb navigation and one `neutron-nav-glyph: dpi=.. button=WxH cell=..`
line per dialog after the first glyph draw.

Compiled clean on both arches (dlls/comdlg32/{x86_64,i386}-windows/itemdlg.o and the
linked comdlg32.dll). Not yet seen on a screen: the 163-dpi arithmetic is from the
comctl32 source, and the after-state needs a capture on a LogPixels=163 prefix.

--- a/dlls/comdlg32/itemdlg.c
+++ b/dlls/comdlg32/itemdlg.c
@@ -19,6 +19,8 @@
  */
 
